<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Zionist Congress</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1897">1897</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Zionist Congress was the inaugural congress of the Zionist Organization, held in the Stadtcasino in Basel on August 29–31, 1897. Convened and chaired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodor-herzl">Theodor Herzl</a>, the founder of the modern Zionist movement, it drew 208 participants from 17 countries, over half of them from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>; planned for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, it was moved to Basel because of opposition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and Reform community leaders. The Congress founded the Zionist Organization and adopted the Basel Program, which stated that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zionism">Zionism</a> sought to establish a home for the Jewish people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> secured under public law. Seventeen women attended but had no vote until the Second Zionist Congress.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-zionist-congress.jpg" alt="First Zionist Congress" width="330" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: לשכת העיתונות הממשלתית · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:THEODOR_HERZL_AT_THE_FIRST_ZIONIST_CONGRESS_IN_BASEL_ON_25.8.1897._תאודור_הרצל_בקונגרס_הציוני_הראשון_-_1897.8.25_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodor-herzl">Theodor Herzl</a> called the Congress as a symbolic parliament for the small minority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> who then supported Zionist aims; most Jews remained indifferent or opposed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zionism">Zionism</a> until after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>. Critics argued that Zionism could not solve the Jewish problem. The Congress opened in the concert hall of the Stadtcasino on Sunday 29 August 1897 and was conducted in German. Of the 208 participants, 69 were delegates of Zionist societies and the rest individual invitees, nearly a quarter of them from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and 26 press correspondents reported on it. The international coverage made a strong impression.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>After a formal opening, with the delegates in white tie, the first day was given to speeches by Karpel Lippe of Jassy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodor-herzl">Herzl</a>, Max Nordau, Nathan Birnbaum and others. Herzl was elected president, with Nordau, Abraham Salz and Samuel Pineles as vice-presidents. On 30 August a committee chaired by Nordau submitted the programme. Many delegates, led by Leo Motzkin, wanted the phrase by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a>, and a compromise formula of Herzl's was adopted. According to Israel Zangwill, Nordau chose the words home secured by public law to avoid antagonising the Ottoman sultan too deeply. The programme named four means: settling Jewish farmers, artisans and tradesmen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, organising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> everywhere, strengthening Jewish national feeling, and seeking the needed governmental consent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Congress absorbed most of the Hovevei Zion societies, which had focused on settlement, and elected an executive with members from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and elsewhere. It proposed a people's bank, which became the Jewish Colonial Trust, and later congresses founded the Jewish National Fund to buy land in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>. On 3 September 1897 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodor-herzl">Herzl</a> wrote in his diary that at Basel he had founded the Jewish State, and that in fifty years everyone would see it. The Congress met yearly until 1901, then mostly every second year, and since the founding of Israel it has met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Zionist Congress” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
